P96Multicast 2.0 - a P96 graphics card that is the network
============================================================

Multicast.card is a Picasso96 (P96) card driver without hardware. Its
video memory is a block of Fast RAM, and a background task sends what is
on screen as UDP packets, by default to the multicast group 239.255.96.1,
port 9696. Watch it on another machine with Client/p96view.py.

New in 2.0: Copper, a protocol of drawing orders. What is drawn (fills,
window moves, scrolling, text, patterns) goes out as a few bytes for the
viewer to draw, pixels only for the rest, and the viewer draws the mouse
pointer. The viewer tells the card what it missed, so nothing is resent
blindly. Use the p96view.py from this package: older viewers speak
protocol 1 only, and Multicast.card 2 no longer does.

Needs: a 68020 or better, P96 (or AROS m68k), and a running TCP/IP stack
with a bsdsocket.library (Riptide, Roadshow, MiamiDx, AmiTCP).

Contents
--------
  MulticastPrefs                   settings and installation (see below)
  Libs/Picasso96/Multicast.card    the driver
  Libs/Picasso96/Wrap.card         experimental: wraps another card driver
  Devs/Monitors/Multicast.info     monitor icon, ToolType BOARDTYPE=Multicast
  Prefs/Multicast.prefs            example settings
  Prefs/Wrap.prefs                 example settings for Wrap.card
  C/mctest                         test tool (see below)
  C/wrapstat                       shows what Wrap.card wraps
  C/MulticastDesktop               the shared desktop, with hotkeys (below)
  C/Boing                          the ball, bouncing over the desktop
  Scripts/                         the desktop without typing (double-click,
                                   or by name in a Shell once installed):
    DesktopOpen                    open it, with a Shell in the middle
    DesktopShell                   another Shell in the middle
    DesktopBoing                   the Boing ball on it
    DesktopClose                   close it
  Client/desktop.command           on a Mac: both halves, a window each
                                   (FS-UAE's NAT; desktop-lan.command for
                                   multicast on a real network)
  Client/p96view.py                viewer for Linux, macOS, Windows (Python 3)
  Client/fake_sender.py            sends a test picture, to try a viewer
                                   without the Amiga
  Docs/                            README, COPPER (the protocol), BUILD,
                                   WRAP (Wrap.card)
  Source/                          the full source

Quick start
-----------
Unpack, then in a Shell, inside this drawer:
  MulticastPrefs INSTALL SETUP=SINGLE      one Multicast screen
  MulticastPrefs INSTALL SETUP=TWO         two Multicast screens
  MulticastPrefs INSTALL SETUP=MIRROR      your display card's screen (e.g.
                                           uaegfx: the Workbench) also on
                                           the network
  MulticastPrefs INSTALL SETUP=SPAN        your display card's screen split
                                           over two Multicast boards, left
                                           and right half: one desktop on two
                                           monitors, drag windows across
                                           (SETUP=SPAN2x2: four)
  MulticastPrefs INSTALL SETUP=DESKTOP     a Multicast screen of its own,
                                           split in two (SPLIT=2x1), a window
                                           for each half: one desktop, drag
                                           windows from one to the other; the
                                           Workbench stays put. After the reboot:
    Run >NIL: MulticastDesktop                   (opens the desktop)
    python3 p96view.py --span 2x1                (on your computer)
                                           (DESKTOP2x2: four windows)
  Or do it all in MulticastPrefs' Desktop page (below).
and say y when it asks to reboot (REBOOT reboots without asking,
NOREBOOT never does). Add DRY to see first what it would do. INSTALL sets up
everything for the next boot: the cards in LIBS:Picasso96, mctest and
wrapstat in C:, MulticastPrefs in SYS:Prefs, the monitor and its icon,
screen modes if Picasso96Mode has none for Multicast, and DEST=10.0.2.2 if
the Amiga is on UAE's NAT network (start the TCP/IP stack before, so it
can tell). It ends with what to run on your computer. Switching setups
later puts back the monitors an earlier one moved aside.

The shared desktop: MulticastPrefs' Desktop page, MulticastDesktop, Boing
-------------------------------------------------------------------------
One Multicast screen shown in parts, a viewer window for each part, like
monitors side by side: windows are dragged from one to the other.

MulticastPrefs, Desktop page (the first): a picture of your monitors.
  Monitors       one, two side by side or one above the other, three or
                 four side by side, four 2 x 2
  Each monitor   its size: 640 x 480 ... 1920 x 1080
  Colours        8, 16 or 32 bit
  Programs open on it   the desktop is the default public screen
  Open at boot   MulticastDesktop in SYS:WBStartup
  Behind Workbench      opens it behind, not in front
  Open now / Close      start or stop it now
Install (or Save, then reboot) sets the card up: the desktop's size as a
mode, enough video memory. On your computer:
  python3 p96view.py --span 2x1 --group 127.0.0.1   (for two side by side)

MulticastDesktop (C:) opens the desktop and keeps it open; a commodity
(Exchange shows it). Hotkeys, for the active window on the desktop:
  ctrl alt cursor keys   to the next monitor left/right/up/down, at the same
                         place on it
  ctrl alt 1 ... 4       to the middle of monitor 1 ... 4
  ctrl alt m             the desktop in front, or the Workbench again
HOTKEYS=ctrl+alt in Multicast.prefs changes the qualifiers.
The Workbench keeps its own display while the desktop is in front with
WBMONITOR=1 ("Workbench own monitor"; SETUP=DESKTOP sets it): Wrap.card
wraps the Workbench's card (uaegfx in FS-UAE) only to keep its display on
(KEEPDISPLAY=1 in Wrap.prefs), and the monitors get DISPLAYCHAIN=NO. For the
mouse to move between the Workbench and the desktop, install P96ScreenCx
(Aminet util/cdity/P96ScreenCx): MulticastPrefs writes the monitor tool
types it reads (RIGHT_OF=... from WORKBENCH=).
The Workbench sits next to the desktop where WORKBENCH= says (LEFT1: left of
monitor 1; RIGHT2, ABOVE1, BELOW2, ...; NONE: the hotkey only), as the
Desktop page's picture shows: click around the monitors to move it. Push
the mouse over that edge and the Workbench comes to the front with the
pointer on its matching edge, and the window you last used there active;
over the Workbench's edge back to the desktop the same way.
From a Shell:
  MulticastDesktop MOVE=RIGHT       (LEFT, UP, DOWN)
  MulticastDesktop CENTRE=2
  MulticastDesktop SWAP
  MulticastDesktop CLOSE            quits it (once no windows are left)
  MulticastDesktop SHELL            a Shell in the middle of the desktop
  MulticastDesktop INFO             the desktop's size and display mode

Boing (C:) bounces the ball over the whole desktop, from monitor to
monitor: Boing [SIZE=100] [SPEED=5] [SECS=n] [PUBSCREEN=name].
Click the ball or Ctrl-C to stop it.

The easy way: MulticastPrefs
----------------------------
Double-click MulticastPrefs (in this package's top drawer, or run it from
a Shell). It sets everything up without P96's own tools:
  Network    where to send, port, rate, updates per second, log file
  Display    boards, video memory, and the screen modes: P96's own
             (Picasso96Mode), or a list of sizes the card adds itself
  Wrap.card  wrap another card, mirror it, or one screen over 2-4 monitors
  Status     what the running cards say, and what is installed
Install copies the cards to LIBS:Picasso96, makes DEVS:Monitors/Multicast
(or Wrap) from a P96 monitor you have, with its icon, moves monitors that
would set a card up twice to SYS:Storage/Monitors, and saves the
settings. Then reboot. Save and Use work as in the system prefs editors.

From a Shell, for scripts:
  MulticastPrefs SHOW                     the settings and their defaults
  MulticastPrefs SET FPS=10 MODES=640x480,1280x960
  MulticastPrefs SET CARD=Multicast.card SPAN=2x2 HEAD=Multicast.card
                                          (also sets BOARDS=5 to match)
  MulticastPrefs INSTALL [SETUP=SINGLE|TWO|MIRROR] [DRY]
  MulticastPrefs STATUS

Installing by hand on P96 (AmigaOS 3.x)
---------------------------------------
1. Copy Libs/Picasso96/Multicast.card to LIBS:Picasso96/
2. Every P96 monitor in DEVS:Monitors is a copy of the same small program
   with an icon that names the board. Copy one you already have, e.g.
     Copy DEVS:Monitors/uaegfx DEVS:Monitors/Multicast
   and copy Devs/Monitors/Multicast.info to DEVS:Monitors/
   (or give the copied monitor's icon the ToolType BOARDTYPE=Multicast).
3. Optional: settings (see below). Without a prefs file the defaults
   are used. To make one:
     Copy Prefs/Multicast.prefs ENVARC:
     Copy Prefs/Multicast.prefs ENV:
   and remove the '#' in front of the lines you change.
4. Reboot. Multicast modes appear in ScreenMode prefs; open a screen in
   one (or use mctest), start your TCP/IP stack as usual.

Installing on AROS m68k
-----------------------
1. Copy Multicast.card to LIBS:Picasso96/
2. Copy DEVS:Monitors/P96 DEVS:Monitors/Multicast  (AROS's P96 monitor
   driver loads the card named after itself; no icon needed)
3. As above for the prefs. AROS may put the Workbench itself on the card.

Settings (ENV:Multicast.prefs, or ToolTypes of the monitor icon)
-----------------------------------------------------------------
  DEST=239.255.96.1   destination, multicast or unicast address
  PORT=9696           UDP port
  TTL=1               multicast TTL (1 = this LAN only)
  FPS=50              update passes per second (1-50)
  RATE=1500           most KB per second to send
  SCAN=2              seconds to compare the whole screen in, at the fastest
  CPU=20              most percent of the CPU that comparing may take; a
                      slow Amiga compares more slowly instead of slowing down
  HISTORY=512         KB of sent data kept, to repair what viewers lost
  REFRESH=10          while no viewer talks back: seconds between whole
                      screens (0 = never)
  POINTER=1           the viewers draw the mouse pointer (0: P96 draws it
                      into the screen)
  INPUT=0             1: the mouse and keys in the viewer's windows work
                      the Amiga (the card's screen comes to the front);
                      anyone who can reach the card could, so it is off
                      unless set; SETUP=DESKTOP sets it. p96view.py
                      --no-input keeps a viewer's to itself
  INPUTFROM=          with INPUT=1: only from this address
  SPLIT=1x1           the screen in CxR parts (at most 4), each sent to a
                      port of its own, PORT, PORT+1, ..., and with a
                      multicast DEST to a group of its own, DEST, DEST+1,
                      ... (239.255.96.1, .2, ...): a window each with
                      p96view.py --span CxR, or each part on another
                      device: p96view.py --part 2
  DEST2= DEST3= DEST4=  where part 2, 3, 4 go instead (e.g. another
                      machine's address)
  VRAM=8              video memory in MB (read at boot)
  MAXWIDTH=1920       largest mode offered
  MAXHEIGHT=1200
  BOARDTYPE=6         P96 board type reported (6 = PicassoII)
  PRI=-1              sender task priority
  LOG=T:Multicast.log log file: stack found, modes, send errors, throughput
  BOARDS=1            boards to offer (1-8): board n sends to PORT+n, its
                      modes are called Multicast2:..., and it logs to LOG.2
                      (P96; AROS takes one board per card, see Wrap.card).
                      VRAM= is for each board
  MODES=...           screen modes the card adds for P96, e.g.
                      MODES=640x480,800x600,1280x960: then no Picasso96Mode
                      setup is needed. Without it, P96's own modes from
                      Picasso96Mode, as before. With both, remove the
                      board's modes in Picasso96Mode, or sizes show twice.
                      AROS uses its own fixed list.
  BLITTER=1           send the drawing as orders (0: compare the screen
                      and send pixels only, as 1.x did)

ENV: wins over ToolTypes. The network settings are read again when the
TCP/IP stack appears, so the card can start before the network.

Testing
-------
  mctest STATUS     is the card loaded, is its sender running, is there a stack
  mctest LIST       list the display modes (look for "Multicast:")
  mctest WIDTH=640 HEIGHT=480 DEPTH=8 SECS=20
                    open a screen on the card with colour bars; depth 8, 16
                    or 32 (16 and 32 need cybergraphics.library)

A second screen on the card, next to your Workbench on another board:
  Run >NIL: mctest PUBSCREEN=MULTICAST WIDTH=800 HEIGHT=600 DEPTH=8 DEFAULT
                    opens a Workbench-like public screen named MULTICAST on
                    the card; with DEFAULT it is the default public screen
                    while open, so new shells and windows go there:
  Add BACK to open it behind your other screens: Intuition shows only
  the front screen on the Amiga's own display, but the card keeps
  streaming its screen while it is behind. Left-Amiga M / N cycles
  screens; the mouse works on whichever screen is in front.
  NewShell "CON:20/20/600/200/Multicast Shell/CLOSE"
                    Programs with a PUBSCREEN option can be pointed at it
                    directly. To close it: close its windows, then
                    Break <process number> (see Status) or Ctrl-C.

On the receiving machine:
  python3 p96view.py                       window; joins 239.255.96.1:9696
                                           (no tkinter, as with Homebrew's
                                           Python: opens a web browser view)
  python3 p96view.py --web                 web browser view, http://127.0.0.1:8096/
  python3 p96view.py --span 2x1            SETUP=SPAN: a window for each board,
                                           like two monitors (ports 9696, 9697);
                                           --one-window puts them in one
  python3 p96view.py --iface 192.168.1.10  join on a particular interface
  python3 p96view.py --snapshot amiga.png --duration 30
  python3 fake_sender.py --dest 127.0.0.1 --seconds 0
                    with "p96view.py --group 127.0.0.1" in another window:
                    checks the viewer without the Amiga
numpy makes the viewer much faster but is optional. The viewer answers the
card (what it lost, the whole picture when it starts) at the address the
packets come from; a firewall must let that through.

Under FS-UAE
------------
* With FS-UAE's built-in network (slirp, uaenet.device unit 0 or the
  A2065), multicast does not reach the host. Send unicast to the host
  instead: DEST=10.0.2.2 in ENV:Multicast.prefs, and on the host
    python3 p96view.py --group 127.0.0.1
  (any non-multicast --group just listens on the port).
* With FS-UAE bridged to your LAN (Riptide's uaenet pcap patch) the
  default multicast works, but the host running FS-UAE cannot see the
  Amiga's frames through the bridge: watch from another machine.

Wrap.card (experimental)
------------------------
Wrap.card loads another card driver and passes every call between it and
rtg.library through, counting the calls and recording what is on display.
It can show the screen on other boards ("heads"): MIRROR= puts the same
screen on a second board, e.g. a Multicast board, so the screen of a real
graphics card goes out on the network too; SPAN=2x2 shows one big screen
on four monitors, a tile on each, so windows are dragged from monitor to
monitor. Docs/WRAP.md has the details.
1. Copy Libs/Picasso96/Wrap.card to LIBS:Picasso96/, next to the real card.
2. P96: set the board's monitor icon ToolType BOARDTYPE=Wrap.
   AROS: Copy DEVS:Monitors/P96 DEVS:Monitors/Wrap, and remove the real
   card's monitor.
3. Copy Prefs/Wrap.prefs to ENVARC: and ENV:, and set CARD= to the real
   card, e.g. CARD=uaegfx.card
4. Reboot, then run wrapstat.
Mirror: add MIRROR=Multicast.card to Wrap.prefs. If CARD= is also
Multicast.card, put BOARDS=2 in Multicast.prefs: the mirror then streams
to port 9697 (python3 p96view.py --port 9697).
Span: SPAN=2x2 and HEAD=<card> (or HEAD1= ... HEAD4=) in Wrap.prefs, then
open a screen twice as wide and high as its mode (1280x960 in a 640x480
mode for 2x2), or use a mode that big. With CARD=Multicast.card and
HEAD=Multicast.card, BOARDS=5: the heads stream to ports 9697-9700.
Try it: mctest SPAN=2x2 WIDTH=640 HEIGHT=480 DEPTH=16
Mirroring to Multicast.card with HEADSHARE=1 (what SETUP=MIRROR sets up),
Wrap.card passes the real card's drawing on, so the mirror sends orders
too. wrapstat says "hears the wrapped board's drawing" for it.

Status
------
Test version. rtg.library still draws with the CPU; the card hears about
it and sends orders. Tested with AROS m68k's P96 layer in FS-UAE with
Riptide (where the viewer's picture is checked against the card's, pixel
for pixel), and with P96 in FS-UAE. mctest STATUS shows what the card
sends: orders, pixels, repairs, viewers.
